## 6. Alt text / accessibility label on images — **does NOT flow to `alt`**

- `LABEL (0x200A)` is a STRING property (`constants.rs:438` + STRING arm at 519; `Properties.java:98`; `ValueTypes.java:26`).
- **Rust HTML renderer**: the IMAGE arm (lib.rs:984–992) emits only `src` + `style` — **no `alt=`, no `aria-label`**. `LABEL` is read only in the `TEXT_FIELD` arm (lib.rs:956–979) where it populates `<span class="pathland-label">`. `aria_attrs` (lib.rs:518–543) handles `ROLE`/`STATE`/`ENABLED` only — never `LABEL`.
- **TS DOM client**: `PROP_LABEL` handler (`apply.ts:585–592`) writes to a `.pathland-label` span or `setNodeText` — never `alt`. The image shell hard-codes `el.alt = ""` (`elements.ts:79`). The only `aria-label` in the client is the renderer-drawn nav back button (`apply.ts:330`).
- **GTK renderer**: `LABEL` is not used for images at all; it only appears as the `TEXT_FIELD` caption (`host.rs:29` comment lists LABEL/PROMPT as resolved strings; used in the TextField arms at lib.rs:349–355 and 977–985).
- `AccessibilityLabel` (→ `LABEL`) applied to an image-bearing `Label` therefore produces an invisible-to-assistive-tech image. **This is a genuine gap**: an image's accessibility text is carried on the wire as `LABEL` but every renderer drops it for `IMAGE` nodes.

